ValueKind <-> typed column mapping (cross-checked against
internal/tesla/protomodel/types.go ValueKind iota block):

  ValueKind             Code   Typed column   Coverage
  --------------------  -----  -------------  ----------------------------
  ValueKindUnknown        0    n/a            never logged (codec drops)
  ValueKindString         1    str_value      TEXT
  ValueKindBool           2    bool_value     BOOLEAN
  ValueKindInt32          3    int_value      BIGINT (widens int32)
  ValueKindInt64          4    int_value      BIGINT (exact)
  ValueKindFloat          5    float_value    DOUBLE PRECISION (widens)
  ValueKindDouble         6    float_value    DOUBLE PRECISION (exact)
  ValueKindEnum           7    int_value      BIGINT (parsed enum number)
  ValueKindCompound       8    n/a            flattened upstream by codec
  ValueKindTime           9    time_value     TIMESTAMPTZ
  ValueKindInvalid       10    n/a            never logged (codec drops)

WHY signal_log is a hypertable while drives/trips/trip_drives (prompt
0033) are not:
  signal_log is the cold-path change feed — every routed Atomic with
  also_signal_log=true plus every Field whose dest is signal_log lands
  here. Row count scales with the telemetry tick rate (~1k atomics/sec
  fleet-wide), not with drive frequency. A 7-day chunk dimension keeps
  recent-window queries fast and lets a future operational prompt add
  compression / retention policies without rewriting the schema.

WHY composite PK (vehicle_id, ts, field) instead of a surrogate id:
  Hypertables require the partitioning column (ts) to be part of every
  unique constraint, so a BIGSERIAL surrogate would still need a
  multi-column unique to enforce exactly-once semantics. The composite
  PK is exactly that constraint, and it is also the most-selective
  read pattern (per-vehicle history of one field) collapsing to an
  index-only scan.

WHY value_kind is SMALLINT:
  protomodel.ValueKind is a Go iota block with values 0..10. SMALLINT
  (-32768..32767) fits the entire ValueKind range with two-byte
  storage and leaves headroom for any future kinds. INTEGER would
  double the per-row overhead with no benefit; CHAR-based encoding
  would require a CHECK constraint and lose the natural ordering.

WHY no FK from signal_log.vehicle_id to vehicles(id):
  Phase-42 prompts 0030-0033 all deliberately omit FKs to vehicles(id)
  on hot/cold-path tables so the writer hot path can insert rows
  without taking a referential lock against the vehicles table. A
  later operational prompt may reinstate the constraint once the
  vehicle ingestion path is settled. signal_log follows that same
  precedent.

WHY no compression / retention policy at this slot:
  The prompt explicitly defers per-table policy decisions to a later
  operational prompt. Phase-42 prompts 0030-0033 followed the same
  rule — none of positions, snapshots, charging_telemetry, drives,
  trips, or trip_drives carries a compression or retention policy yet.

WHY the legacy CAGGs are dropped (and not migrated):
  cagg_vehicle_daily / cagg_climate_hourly / cagg_battery_daily
  reference the legacy signal_log columns (created_at, signal,
  value_num, value_str, value_bool) which do not exist in the new
  schema (ts, field, value_kind, float_value, str_value, bool_value).
  The CAGGs cannot be silently retargeted — Postgres would error on
  the missing column, blocking the migration. Forward-only rewrite
  per ADR-004 means we drop them; a later operational prompt may
  redefine equivalent CAGGs over the new schema once the routing.yaml
  field set is finalized.

  cagg_signal_hourly references signal_observations.value_numeric, so
  it is dropped together with the cold-path tall table that backed it.

migrations/000173_signal_log.down.sql:
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS signal_log CASCADE;
  (Forward-only rollback — there is no legacy schema to restore. The
  rollback does NOT recreate signal_observations / signal_catalog /
  the legacy CAGGs; per ADR-004 these are deleted irreversibly.)

